1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,109
This is STAT 331, video 17 b. It continues video 17 a, and uses its cards A and B.

2
00:00:09,559 --> 00:00:15,853
Type B again: confidence and prediction intervals, now at many covariate rows at once.

3
00:00:16,303 --> 00:00:22,532
Type C: answer questions from regression output, as in practice question 1 of Lecture 8.

4
00:00:22,982 --> 00:00:30,534
Type D: show results for a model with two blocks of covariates, X and Z, as in practice question 2.

5
00:00:30,984 --> 00:00:37,704
Type E: check the coverage of a prediction interval by simulation, as in practice question 3.

6
00:00:38,154 --> 00:00:56,821
Section 1. Often we want intervals at many covariate rows, for example to plot a trend. Stack K new rows into a matrix X new, with K rows and p plus 1 columns. The predictions are X new times beta hat. This is step 1 of card A.

7
00:00:57,271 --> 00:00:59,596
Step 2: the mean is X new beta.

8
00:01:00,046 --> 00:01:09,134
Step 3: the variance is X new, times the variance of beta hat, times X new transpose. The picture shows the sizes.

9
00:01:09,584 --> 00:01:13,531
Step 4: put in sigma squared X transpose X inverse.

10
00:01:13,981 --> 00:01:16,477
The product is a K by K matrix.

11
00:01:16,927 --> 00:01:26,889
We need only the variance of each prediction. That is the k-th diagonal entry. The other entries are covariances between predictions.

12
00:01:27,339 --> 00:01:38,070
So the diagonal gives h for every row at once. Then card B gives the interval for each row. The R code on slide 46 does exactly this.

13
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:53,432
A worked problem of type B with many rows, from slides 46 and 47. Pause the video and try it. Age and stress stay at their means. Severity takes five values from 41 to 62.

14
00:01:53,882 --> 00:02:00,047
Step 1 of card B: each row of X new is 1, age, the severity value, and stress.

15
00:02:00,497 --> 00:02:07,857
Step 2: the predictions. They go down as severity goes up, because the severity coefficient is negative.

16
00:02:08,307 --> 00:02:15,923
Step 3: h for each row, from the diagonal of X new, X transpose X inverse, X new transpose.

17
00:02:16,373 --> 00:02:40,928
Steps 4 to 6 for row 1, the confidence interval. 2.018 times 10.058 is 20.297. The square root of h, 0.2347, is 0.4845. The half-width is 9.834. The interval is 55.90 to 75.57.

18
00:02:41,378 --> 00:02:56,823
The prediction interval of row 1 uses the square root of 1 plus h, 1.1112. The half-width is 22.554. The interval is 43.18 to 88.29.

19
00:02:57,273 --> 00:03:04,121
The other rows use the same steps. All numbers match the tables on slides 46 and 47.

20
00:03:04,571 --> 00:03:17,435
The bars compare the half-widths. The confidence interval half-width changes a lot with h. The prediction interval half-width is always at least t times sigma hat, which is 20.3.

21
00:03:17,885 --> 00:03:27,613
Now draw the estimate as a line over severity. Age and stress stay at their means. The five green points are the five rows of the table.

22
00:03:28,063 --> 00:03:42,698
Compute the confidence interval at every severity value. The green band is the pointwise 95 percent confidence band. It answers: where is the mean satisfaction of all patients with these covariates?

23
00:03:43,148 --> 00:03:54,433
The purple band is the 95 percent prediction band. It answers: where is the satisfaction of one new patient? It is much wider, because it adds the new error.

24
00:03:54,883 --> 00:04:05,187
Both bands are narrowest near the mean severity, 50.4. Far from the center of the data, h is larger, so both intervals are wider.

25
00:04:05,637 --> 00:04:17,605
Section 2. Question type C gives regression output and asks questions about it. Practice question 1 of Lecture 8 is an example. Method card C has six steps.

26
00:04:18,055 --> 00:04:29,255
Step 1: read the output. Each row gives an estimate and its standard error. The last line gives sigma hat and its degrees of freedom, n minus p plus 1.

27
00:04:29,705 --> 00:04:35,465
Step 2: the sample size. n equals the degrees of freedom plus p plus 1.

28
00:04:35,915 --> 00:04:51,019
Step 3: the diagonal of X transpose X inverse. We call it V. The standard error is sigma hat times the square root of V j j. So V j j is the standard error over sigma hat, squared.

29
00:04:51,469 --> 00:05:04,845
Step 4: a test of beta j equals a value b. t obs is beta j hat minus b, over the standard error. The rejection region is the tail that the alternative points to.

30
00:05:05,295 --> 00:05:14,063
Step 5: a confidence interval for beta j: the estimate plus or minus the t quantile times the standard error.

31
00:05:14,513 --> 00:05:26,673
Step 6: a sum of two coefficients. Its variance needs the covariance entry V j k. The output does not give covariances. Then there is not enough information.

32
00:05:27,123 --> 00:05:34,590
Practice question 1, parts a and b. Pause the video and try it. Part a asks for the number of observations.

33
00:05:35,040 --> 00:05:46,475
Step 1 of card C: the last line says 13 degrees of freedom. The degrees of freedom are n minus p plus 1. There are 3 covariates, so p is 3.

34
00:05:46,925 --> 00:05:51,703
Step 2: n equals 13 plus 3 plus 1, which is 17.

35
00:05:52,153 --> 00:06:04,783
Part b. Step 3 of card C. The rows and columns of V start at beta 0. So the second diagonal entry is V 1 1, the entry for beta 1, TEMP.

36
00:06:05,233 --> 00:06:11,249
The standard error of beta 1 hat is sigma hat times the square root of V 1 1.

37
00:06:11,699 --> 00:06:31,176
So V 1 1 is the standard error, 0.0659507, divided by sigma hat, 3.543, all squared. 0.018614 squared is 3.465 times 10 to the minus 4.

38
00:06:31,626 --> 00:06:47,647
Part c. Pause the video and try it. The null value is 0.2. The output t value, 1.525, is for the null value 0, so we compute t obs ourselves. This is step 4 of card C.

39
00:06:48,097 --> 00:07:11,820
t obs is beta 1 hat minus 0.2, over the standard error. 0.1005776 minus 0.2 is minus 0.0994224. Divided by 0.0659507, this is minus 1.508.

40
00:07:12,270 --> 00:07:30,766
The alternative is beta 1 less than 0.2, so the rejection region is the left tail. The critical value is the 0.01 quantile of t with 13 degrees of freedom: minus 2.650. The red area shows the region.

41
00:07:31,216 --> 00:07:41,072
t obs, minus 1.508, is greater than minus 2.650. The blue line is outside the red region.

42
00:07:41,522 --> 00:07:50,717
The one-sided p-value is the area to the left of t obs: 0.078. It is more than 0.01.

43
00:07:51,167 --> 00:07:56,415
So we do not reject the null hypothesis at the 0.01 level.

44
00:07:56,865 --> 00:08:14,571
Part d. Pause the video and try it. Step 5 of card C. A 90 percent interval has alpha equal to 0.1. So we need the 0.95 quantile of t with 13 degrees of freedom: 1.771.

45
00:08:15,021 --> 00:08:31,640
The interval is beta 2 hat plus or minus 1.771 times the standard error. Beta 2 hat is 0.0008046. On the number line, the unit is 10 to the minus 3.

46
00:08:32,090 --> 00:08:43,034
1.771 times 0.0002527 is 0.0004475.

47
00:08:43,484 --> 00:08:52,508
Subtract and add. The interval is 0.000357 to 0.001252.

48
00:08:52,958 --> 00:08:59,038
Zero is not in the interval. This agrees with the small p-value of PROD in the output.

49
00:08:59,488 --> 00:09:10,453
So the 90 percent confidence interval for beta 2 is 0.000357 to 0.001252.

50
00:09:10,903 --> 00:09:21,293
Part e. Pause the video and try it. Step 6 of card C. The variance of a sum is the sum of the variances, plus 2 times the covariance.

51
00:09:21,743 --> 00:09:32,089
With the variance of beta hat equal to sigma squared V, this is sigma squared times V 1 1, plus V 3 3, plus 2 V 1 3.

52
00:09:32,539 --> 00:09:39,963
V 1 1 and V 3 3 come from the standard errors, as in part b. The green cells show them.

53
00:09:40,413 --> 00:09:45,021
V 1 3 is the covariance term. The output does not give it.

54
00:09:45,471 --> 00:09:49,141
So we do not have enough information to build this interval.

55
00:09:49,591 --> 00:10:02,668
Section 3. Question type D uses a model with two blocks of covariates, X and Z. Practice question 2 of Lecture 8 is an example. Method card D has five steps.

56
00:10:03,118 --> 00:10:17,539
Step 1: put X and Z side by side in one design matrix W. Stack beta and alpha into one vector theta. Then y equals W theta plus epsilon, a usual linear model.

57
00:10:17,989 --> 00:10:29,040
Step 2: the least squares estimator is W transpose W inverse, times W transpose y. Write W transpose W in blocks.

58
00:10:29,490 --> 00:10:42,887
Step 3: X transpose Z is 0. The off-diagonal blocks are 0, so W transpose W is block diagonal. Invert each block, and read beta hat and alpha hat.

59
00:10:43,337 --> 00:10:52,937
Step 4: for independence, find the covariance. For A y and B y, it is A, times the variance of y, times B transpose.

60
00:10:53,387 --> 00:11:05,462
Step 5: both estimators come from one matrix times the normal vector y, so they are jointly normal. Jointly normal with zero covariance means independent.

61
00:11:05,912 --> 00:11:20,248
Practice question 2, part a. Pause the video and try it. The model has two blocks of covariates, X and Z. The columns of X are orthogonal to the columns of Z: X transpose Z is 0.

62
00:11:20,698 --> 00:11:32,239
Step 1 of card D: put X and Z side by side in W, and stack beta and alpha into theta. Then y equals W theta plus epsilon.

63
00:11:32,689 --> 00:11:42,268
Step 2: this is a usual linear model, so theta hat equals W transpose W inverse, times W transpose y.

64
00:11:42,718 --> 00:11:53,769
Multiply W transpose W in blocks. The blocks are X transpose X, X transpose Z, Z transpose X, and Z transpose Z.

65
00:11:54,219 --> 00:12:05,376
Step 3: X transpose Z is 0. Z transpose X is its transpose, so it is 0 too. W transpose W is block diagonal.

66
00:12:05,826 --> 00:12:12,098
The inverse of a block diagonal matrix is block diagonal, with the inverse of each block.

67
00:12:12,548 --> 00:12:19,823
W transpose y also splits in blocks: X transpose y on top of Z transpose y.

68
00:12:20,273 --> 00:12:31,323
Multiply. The top block is X transpose X inverse, X transpose y. The bottom block is Z transpose Z inverse, Z transpose y.

69
00:12:31,773 --> 00:12:39,603
The top part of theta hat is beta hat, and the bottom part is alpha hat. This is what part a asks us to show.

70
00:12:40,053 --> 00:12:46,623
Part b. Pause the video and try it. We must show that beta hat and alpha hat are independent.

71
00:12:47,073 --> 00:12:55,009
Step 4 of card D. From part a, beta hat is A y and alpha hat is B y, with the matrices shown.

72
00:12:55,459 --> 00:13:02,286
The covariance of A y and B y is A, times the variance of y, times B transpose.

73
00:13:02,736 --> 00:13:13,083
The variance of y is sigma squared I. B transpose is Z, times Z transpose Z inverse, because Z transpose Z is symmetric.

74
00:13:13,533 --> 00:13:18,247
Sigma squared moves to the front. In the middle we get X transpose Z.

75
00:13:18,697 --> 00:13:26,932
X transpose Z is 0. So the covariance matrix is 0. The red box in the picture shows this middle part.

76
00:13:27,382 --> 00:13:39,414
Step 5. Stack beta hat on alpha hat. The stack is one matrix times y, and y is multivariate normal. So the two estimators are jointly normal.

77
00:13:39,864 --> 00:13:44,579
For jointly normal vectors, zero covariance means independence.

78
00:13:45,029 --> 00:13:47,930
So beta hat and alpha hat are independent.

79
00:13:48,380 --> 00:14:02,652
Section 4. Question type E checks the meaning of a 95 percent prediction interval by simulation. Practice question 3 of Lecture 8 is an example. Method card E has five steps.

80
00:14:03,102 --> 00:14:10,099
Step 1: simulate the responses from the true model. The purple line is the true mean at the new point.

81
00:14:10,549 --> 00:14:20,021
Step 2: fit the model and compute the 95 percent prediction interval at the new point, with card B. The green bar is the interval.

82
00:14:20,471 --> 00:14:29,047
Step 3: draw a new response at the new point. It is covered if it is inside the interval. The blue dot is the new response.

83
00:14:29,497 --> 00:14:37,433
Step 4: repeat 2000 times. The coverage is the number of covered repeats, divided by 2000.

84
00:14:37,883 --> 00:14:47,377
Step 5: say what changes in each repeat. In part e, the data and the new response change. In part f, only the data change.

85
00:14:47,827 --> 00:15:03,144
Practice question 3 is a simulation. Pause the video and try it. The true model is known, with variance 2. There are 10 observations, and the new point is x 1 equals 5.5 and x 2 equals 20.

86
00:15:03,594 --> 00:15:19,445
First the true mean at the new point: 1 plus 0.9 times 5.5 plus 2 times 20. That is 1 plus 4.95 plus 40, which is 45.95. The purple line shows it.

87
00:15:19,895 --> 00:15:27,425
Part a, step 1 of card E: simulate the 10 responses from the true model. The row y shows our run.

88
00:15:27,875 --> 00:15:45,859
Part b, step 2: fit the model. Beta hat is 2.1369, 0.6319 and 2.0102. Sigma hat is 1.0319, with 10 minus 3, which is 7, degrees of freedom.

89
00:15:46,309 --> 00:16:02,267
Part c uses card B. The prediction is 2.137 plus 0.6319 times 5.5, plus 2.0102 times 20. That is 45.817.

90
00:16:02,717 --> 00:16:17,245
h is 0.2567, so the square root of 1 plus h is 1.1210. The t quantile with 7 degrees of freedom is 2.3646.

91
00:16:17,695 --> 00:16:29,236
The half-width is 2.3646 times 1.0319 times 1.1210, which is 2.735.

92
00:16:29,686 --> 00:16:39,137
So the 95 percent prediction interval is 43.08 to 48.55. The green bar shows it.

93
00:16:39,587 --> 00:16:50,872
Part d, step 3 of card E: draw a new response from the true model. It is 46.04. It is inside the interval, so the interval covers it.

94
00:16:51,322 --> 00:17:09,135
Part e. Pause the video and try it. Step 4 of card E: repeat parts a to d 2000 times. Each repeat has new data, a new interval, and a new y new. The picture shows the first 20 repeats. A green interval covers its new value.

95
00:17:09,585 --> 00:17:14,023
A red interval misses its new value. Here 0 of the 20 miss.

96
00:17:14,473 --> 00:17:25,609
Over 2000 repeats, 1910 intervals cover the new observation. That is 0.955, close to 0.95.

97
00:17:26,059 --> 00:17:36,064
Step 5 of card E: in part e, both the data and the new response change in each repeat. The 95 percent refers to this.

98
00:17:36,514 --> 00:17:50,210
Part f. Pause the video and try it. Draw the new response once, and keep it fixed. In our run it is 45.95. The blue line shows it. Only the data change in each repeat.

99
00:17:50,660 --> 00:18:02,052
Step 4: over 2000 repeats, 1998 intervals cover it. The coverage is 0.999. This is not 0.95.

100
00:18:02,502 --> 00:18:13,851
Step 5: the coverage now depends on where the fixed value is. The green curve shows the coverage for each fixed value. The red line is 0.95.

101
00:18:14,301 --> 00:18:22,728
Our fixed value is near the true mean, so its coverage is near 1. A fixed value far from the mean has a low coverage.

102
00:18:23,178 --> 00:18:37,770
Average the green curve over the density of y new, the purple curve. The result is 0.95 again, which is part e. So the 95 percent is over new data and a new y new together.

103
00:18:38,220 --> 00:18:52,129
To recap. Type B with many rows: stack the rows into X new. The diagonal of X new, X transpose X inverse, X new transpose gives h for each row. Then use card B.

104
00:18:52,579 --> 00:19:10,094
Type C, regression output: n is the degrees of freedom plus p plus 1. V j j is the standard error over sigma hat, squared. Tests and intervals use t with the output degrees of freedom. A sum of coefficients needs the covariance.

105
00:19:10,544 --> 00:19:26,672
Type D, two blocks: stack them in W. X transpose Z equal to 0 makes W transpose W block diagonal, and makes the covariance 0. Jointly normal with zero covariance means independent.

106
00:19:27,122 --> 00:19:41,949
Type E, coverage: with new data and a new y new each time, the coverage is close to 0.95. With one fixed y new, it is not. The next video reviews exam problems for Midterm 1.
